1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:06,773
A I increasingly writes and grades its own training data, through self-rewarding pipelines, constitutional loops, and synthetic data.

2
00:00:06,773 --> 00:00:13,447
Once a model helps choose what it trains on, its values can drift from one round to the next.

3
00:00:13,447 --> 00:00:18,364
Value dynamics asks where they drift to, and whether you can tell in advance.

4
00:00:18,364 --> 00:00:22,228
Each round of a judging loop is one generation of selection.

5
00:00:22,228 --> 00:00:32,766
The candidates a model writes are a population that varies, the judge keeps some and discards the rest, and training carries the kept answers into the model that comes next.

6
00:00:32,766 --> 00:00:37,684
Population genetics has measured that structure for a century, so I took its tools.

7
00:00:37,684 --> 00:00:46,816
The difference in means between the kept candidates and the whole pool is a selection differential, and the Price equation tracks how selection changes a population.

8
00:00:48,066 --> 00:00:55,048
I built two model organisms, one fine-tuned to prefer risky gambles and one fine-tuned to write insecure code.

9
00:00:55,048 --> 00:00:58,927
Each round, an organism writes several candidate answers per prompt.

10
00:00:58,927 --> 00:01:03,969
The judge compares each against an alternative and keeps the ones it prefers.

11
00:01:03,969 --> 00:01:09,787
Those become the training data, and held-out prompts re-measure the value before the next round.

12
00:01:09,787 --> 00:01:16,381
Across runs I varied the judge, where the candidates came from, and what they were compared against.

13
00:01:17,131 --> 00:01:20,800
An organism's value is the mean value score of its answers.

14
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:26,136
For the gambling organism, that score is the share of answers that pick the risky gamble.

15
00:01:26,136 --> 00:01:34,808
For the insecure-code organism, it is how insecure its answers to three fixed questions about its own coding habits read, scored by its frozen base model.

16
00:01:35,558 --> 00:01:45,746
The breeder's equation relates that differential to the response in the next generation, so what I need each round is how much the candidates vary and which way the judge sorts them.

17
00:01:45,746 --> 00:01:51,476
Spread is the standard deviation of the candidates' value scores within a prompt, averaged over the round's prompts.

18
00:01:51,476 --> 00:01:55,297
Agreement is the correlation between the judge's preferences and those same scores.

19
00:01:55,297 --> 00:02:04,530
Multiply the two and you get a forecast of the gap between the kept answers and the pool average, and it holds across every round with logged judge scores.

20
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:07,709
The one-round rule has no fitted coefficient.

21
00:02:07,709 --> 00:02:12,915
The next measured value is the mean value score of the candidates the judge kept.

22
00:02:12,915 --> 00:02:19,855
Held out one complete experimental condition at a time, it predicts that next value well ahead of assuming no change.

23
00:02:20,605 --> 00:02:25,969
Before the judge runs, the forecast substitutes spread times agreement for the kept mean.

24
00:02:25,969 --> 00:02:32,865
On matched rounds that costs a little accuracy, the price of predicting selection instead of watching it happen.

25
00:02:32,865 --> 00:02:41,294
To reach an endpoint, the model repeats that update from the first round's candidate mean, holding spread, agreement, and pool composition fixed.

26
00:02:42,044 --> 00:02:52,767
That iteration predicts a run's final value from its first round with a mean absolute error of zero point one one eight, against zero point four three one for assuming no change.

27
00:02:52,767 --> 00:02:55,783
Nothing measured after the first round goes back in.

28
00:02:56,933 --> 00:03:04,751
On the self-only runs, where every candidate came from the organism, the recurrence again lands much closer than assuming no change.

29
00:03:04,751 --> 00:03:08,102
And it holds up as it looks further ahead.

30
00:03:08,102 --> 00:03:14,059
Its error grows only slightly as the horizon lengthens, while assuming no change gets steadily worse.

31
00:03:14,059 --> 00:03:18,899
Selection moves a run mostly in its first rounds and then levels off.

32
00:03:19,649 --> 00:03:24,227
The deterministic forecast gives only the average path real runs scatter around.

33
00:03:24,227 --> 00:03:29,568
Each value reading comes from a limited sample of answers, so it carries noise.

34
00:03:29,568 --> 00:03:39,868
The judge's picks land around spread times agreement rather than exactly on it, training lands near but not on the kept mean, and agreement drifts between rounds.

35
00:03:39,868 --> 00:03:45,591
The stochastic version adds a random term at each point, sized from the measured residuals.

36
00:03:46,341 --> 00:03:49,247
The stochastic model reproduces the observed dynamics.

37
00:03:49,247 --> 00:04:00,043
Simulated runs accumulate about as much round-to-round value change as the real ones, change direction about as often, and scatter about as widely at the end.

38
00:04:00,043 --> 00:04:05,440
Eighty-nine percent of observed final values fall inside the model's eighty percent bands.

39
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:08,475
Both interventions targeted spread and agreement.

40
00:04:08,475 --> 00:04:16,474
Adding base-model answers to a collapsed pool restored spread, and the judge's agreement then eroded a value that had been stuck.

41
00:04:16,474 --> 00:04:26,758
Swapping the base-model judge for a min-risk oracle, which drives agreement to its negative extreme, reversed a run that had climbed near the top of the scale.

42
00:04:26,758 --> 00:04:29,043
Each result is a single experiment.

43
00:04:29,793 --> 00:04:40,708
The training setup here is narrow: small models, short runs, filtered supervised fine-tuning, a handful of model families, and the risk-preference and insecure-code behaviors.

44
00:04:40,708 --> 00:04:52,077
Extensions should scale that up and compare this fine-tuning update with D P O, online reinforcement learning against a learned reward model, and constitutional feedback.

45
00:04:52,077 --> 00:05:02,082
The behaviors should widen to moral judgment, A I identity, and emergent misalignment, with readouts that reach past answers into internal representations.

46
00:05:02,082 --> 00:05:11,178
More open-ended setups would give models more freedom in selecting training data, revising system prompts, and editing the loop itself.

47
00:05:11,178 --> 00:05:19,818
Repeated games and agentic environments could reveal whether the dynamics favor cooperation or defection, resource grabbing, and reward hacking.

48
00:05:19,818 --> 00:05:33,916
Natural and cultural selection sculpted human values, and value dynamics research can help identify artificial selection mechanisms that can be engineered into virtuous cycles for aligning increasingly autonomous A I systems.
